e,"row_header":true,"row_section":false,"row_span":1,"start_col_offset_idx":0,"start_row_offset_idx":9,"text":"Probability"},{"bbox":{"b":313.9989130496979,"coord_origin":"TOPLEFT","l":359.60966444015503,"r":395.39986205101013,"t":302.10887610912323},"col_span":1,"column_header":false,"end_col_offset_idx":2,"end_row_offset_idx":10,"fillable":false,"row_header":false,"row_section":false,"row_span":1,"start_col_offset_idx":1,"start_row_offset_idx":9,"text":"0.000000"},{"bbox":{"b":313.7449826002121,"coord_origin":"TOPLEFT","l":402.70171666145325,"r":436.5826051235199,"t":302.24161291122437},"col_span":1,"column_header":false,"end_col_offset_idx":3,"end_row_offset_idx":10,"fillable":false,"row_header":false,"row_section":false,"row_span":1,"start_col_offset_idx":2,"start_row_offset_idx":9,"text":" 0.000000"},{"bbox":{"b":328.398865878582,"coord_origin":"TOPLEFT","l":248.6407168507576,"r":334.54566007852554,"t":317.1953528523445},"col_span":1,"column_header":false,"end_col_offset_idx":1,"end_row_offset_idx":11,"fillable":false,"row_header":true,"row_section":false,"row_span":1,"start_col_offset_idx":0,"start_row_offset_idx":10,"text":"Number of observations"},{"bbox":{"b":328.59946846961975,"coord_origin":"TOPLEFT","l":373.1698782444,"r":394.596143245697,"t":316.6972818374634},"col_span":1,"column_header":false,"end_col_offset_idx":2,"end_row_offset_idx":11,"fillable":false,"row_header":false,"row_section":false,"row_span":1,"start_col_offset_idx":1,"start_row_offset_idx":10,"text":"4703"},{"bbox":{"b":328.5903135538101,"coord_origin":"TOPLEFT","l":416.40733897686005,"r":437.13601315021515,"t":316.51109397411346},"col_span":1,"column_header":false,"end_col_offset_idx":3,"end_row_offset_idx":11,"fillable":false,"row_header":false,"row_section":false,"row_span":1,"start_col_offset_idx":2,"start_row_offset_idx":10,"text":"4703"}]},"footnotes":[],"label":"table","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":350.3055114746094,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":246.40110778808594,"r":440.03167724609375,"t":509.79991149902344},"charspan":[0,0],"page_no":1}],"references":[],"self_ref":"#/tables/0"}],"texts":[{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_header","orig":"Mile Bošnjak, Vlatka Bilas, Ivan Novak: Modeling exchange rate volatilities in Croatia","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":634.3922054235135,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":42.5197,"r":298.3647999999997,"t":641.6602024414062},"charspan":[0,86],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/0","text":"Mile Bošnjak, Vlatka Bilas, Ivan Novak: Modeling exchange rate volatilities in Croatia"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"In accordance to Lang (2005), exchange rate volatility  is  the  main  driving  force  of  Croatian  foreign exchange  intervention  while  the exchange  rate level  turns  out  to  be  insignificant.  According  to Chmelarova and Schnabl (2006), Croatian foreign exchange  intervention  manages  both  day-to-day exchange  rate  volatilities  as  well  as  exchange  rate levels. Furthermore, the authors concluded that the pattern of foreign exchange intervention for Croatia confirms a fear of depreciation (with respect to balance  sheet  effects  of  the  banking  sector)  more than a fear of appreciation (with respect to export competitiveness).  Following  the  previous  research results and empirical data on exchange rate movements,  the  EUR/HRK  exchange  rate  might  be strictly  controlled  by  the  Croatian  National  Bank. Since  the  Croatian  National  Bank  cannot  control the USD/HRK exchange rate at the same time, it's interesting to compare the EUR/HRK and the USD/ HRK volatilities pattern.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":401.3645992835118,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":42.5197,"r":237.08170000000007,"t":615.8232024414062},"charspan":[0,1019],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/1","text":"In accordance to Lang (2005), exchange rate volatility  is  the  main  driving  force  of  Croatian  foreign exchange  intervention  while  the exchange  rate level  turns  out  to  be  insignificant.  According  to Chmelarova and Schnabl (2006), Croatian foreign exchange  intervention  manages  both  day-to-day exchange  rate  volatilities  as  well  as  exchange  rate levels. Furthermore, the authors concluded that the pattern of foreign exchange intervention for Croatia confirms a fear of depreciation (with respect to balance  sheet  effects  of  the  banking  sector)  more than a fear of appreciation (with respect to export competitiveness).  Following  the  previous  research results and empirical data on exchange rate movements,  the  EUR/HRK  exchange  rate  might  be strictly  controlled  by  the  Croatian  National  Bank. Since  the  Croatian  National  Bank  cannot  control the USD/HRK exchange rate at the same time, it's interesting to compare the EUR/HRK and the USD/ HRK volatilities pattern."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"section_header","level":1,"orig":"4. Research data","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":357.4208676587975,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":42.5197,"r":112.74970000000002,"t":365.9363024414062},"charspan":[0,16],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/2","text":"4. Research data"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Financial time series often exhibit volatility clustering, meaning that high volatility periods tend to be followed by high volatility periods and low volatility periods tend to be followed by low volatility periods. In that case, a strong autocorrelation in squared returns  or  autoregressive  conditional  heteroscedasticity is present. As a consequence, the least squares estimators are still unbiased but inefficient. The estimates of the variances are biased, thus invalidating the tests of significance, and the obtained results are dubious (see for example Erjavec and Cota, 2007). In order to resolve the problem and obtain estimator  efficiency,  as  a  method  of  estimation  several ARCH type models has been employed. As in most empirical finance literature, the variable to be modeled is the daily exchange rate return which is the first  difference  of  the  natural  logarithm  of  the  exchange rate and is given by the following equation:","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":146.5566992835113,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":42.5197,"r":237.08710000000005,"t":339.4153024414062},"charspan":[0,957],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/3","text":"Financial time series often exhibit volatility clustering, meaning that high volatility periods tend to be followed by high volatility periods and low volatility periods tend to be followed by low volatility periods. In that case, a strong autocorrelation in squared returns  or  autoregressive  conditional  heteroscedasticity is present. As a consequence, the least squares estimators are still unbiased but inefficient. The estimates of the variances are biased, thus invalidating the tests of significance, and the obtained results are dubious (see for example Erjavec and Cota, 2007). In order to resolve the problem and obtain estimator  efficiency,  as  a  method  of  estimation  several ARCH type models has been employed. As in most empirical finance literature, the variable to be modeled is the daily exchange rate return which is the first  difference  of  the  natural  logarithm  of  the  exchange rate and is given by the following equation:"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"formula","orig":"(1)","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":101.40369928351129,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":222.52149999999997,"r":232.90749999999997,"t":110.66230244140604},"charspan":[0,3],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/4","text":""},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Where r t is the daily exchange rate return and S t and S t -1 denote the Croatian National Bank (CNB) midpoint exchange rate of the EUR versus the HRK and the USD versus the HRK at the current day and pre- vious day, respectively. The data span from 1 st January 1997 to 30 th September 2015 is used as a data sample for modeling the daily exchange rate return. Table 1 shows the descriptive statistics for the observed variables.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":53.94889928351142,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":42.516200000000026,"r":237.07770000000005,"t":95.61430244140604},"charspan":[0,206],"page_no":1},{"bbox":{"b":563.3645992835117,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":246.93770000000004,"r":441.49930000000035,"t":616.3469754414061},"charspan":[207,431],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/5","text":"Where r t is the daily exchange rate return and S t and S t -1 denote the Croatian National Bank (CNB) midpoint exchange rate of the EUR versus the HRK and the USD versus the HRK at the current day and pre- vious day, respectively. The data span from 1 st January 1997 to 30 th September 2015 is used as a data sample for modeling the daily exchange rate return. Table 1 shows the descriptive statistics for the observed variables."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"caption","orig":"Table 1 Descriptive statistics for the daily exchange rates return of the EUR and the USD versus the HRK","parent":{"$ref":"#/tables/0"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":515.8306348469731,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":246.9472,"r":428.11359999999985,"t":546.7752024414065},"charspan":[0,104],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/6","text":"Table 1 Descriptive statistics for the daily exchange rates return of the EUR and the USD versus the HRK"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"Source: Authors' calculation","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":329.9299992835115,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":246.9449,"r":351.02810000000034,"t":339.1886024414062},"charspan":[0,28],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/7","text":"Source: Authors' calculation"},{"children":[],"content_layer":"body","label":"text","orig":"A key feature of exchange rate returns is that the distribution of returns is fat tailed. That is, the probability density function of exchange rate returns  appears to be leptokurtic, so it is more peaked at the center and has fatter tails compared to that of the normal distribution. Numerically, the kurtosis coefficient is found to be greater than 3, which characterizes kurtosis of a normally distributed random variable. In addition, exchange rate returns tend to be slightly skewed either to the left or to the right which is not consistent with returns being normally distributed. The positive value of skewness indicates that data are skewed to the right referring to a depreciation of the currency (HRK). As can be seen in Table 1, the exchange rate returns series ( r t ) exhibits  significant  values  of  skewness  and  kurtosis, and therefore normality assumptions are not met. In accordance to abovementioned alternative, distributions have to be used as a basis for modeling exchange rate returns, such as the Student-t or Generalized  Error  distribution  (GED)  rather  than  the normal  distribution  which  takes  into  account  the phenomenon of leptokurtosis and skewness in the probability density function.","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":55.67799928351144,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":246.94339999999997,"r":441.51410000000016,"t":313.3406024414062},"charspan":[0,1230],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/8","text":"A key feature of exchange rate returns is that the distribution of returns is fat tailed. That is, the probability density function of exchange rate returns  appears to be leptokurtic, so it is more peaked at the center and has fatter tails compared to that of the normal distribution. Numerically, the kurtosis coefficient is found to be greater than 3, which characterizes kurtosis of a normally distributed random variable. In addition, exchange rate returns tend to be slightly skewed either to the left or to the right which is not consistent with returns being normally distributed. The positive value of skewness indicates that data are skewed to the right referring to a depreciation of the currency (HRK). As can be seen in Table 1, the exchange rate returns series ( r t ) exhibits  significant  values  of  skewness  and  kurtosis, and therefore normality assumptions are not met. In accordance to abovementioned alternative, distributions have to be used as a basis for modeling exchange rate returns, such as the Student-t or Generalized  Error  distribution  (GED)  rather  than  the normal  distribution  which  takes  into  account  the phenomenon of leptokurtosis and skewness in the probability density function."},{"children":[],"content_layer":"furniture","label":"page_footer","orig":"84 God. XXIX, BR. 1/2016. str. 81-94","parent":{"$ref":"#/body"},"prov":[{"bbox":{"b":35.35006665193248,"coord_origin":"BOTTOMLEFT","l":54.0695,"r":309.6725999999998,"t":43.417002441406225},"charspan":[0,36],"page_no":1}],"self_ref":"#/texts/9","text":"84 God. XXIX, BR. 1/2016. str. 81-94"}],"version":"1.10.0"}